  "subtitle": "The first system to use a live actor's real face as a real-time input device for computer-generated animation",
  "why_scout_cares": "Lance Williams' 1990 SIGGRAPH paper introduced the 'electronic mask' — covering an actor's face with retroreflective dots, tracking their 3D positions in real time with computer vision, and mapping the movements directly onto a photorealistic 3D CG face. This was the birth of facial performance capture: the idea that nuanced human expression could be a real-time input stream, not a laborious keyframing exercise. Decades before Avatar or ARKit, Williams showed that an actor's smile, frown, or raised eyebrow could drive a digital character instantly. The system ran at interactive rates on late-1980s Apple ATG hardware, used a Cyberware 3D color scanner to build the face model, and established the fundamental pipeline that still underlies modern VFX performance capture. This is a case where a research system — not a product — changed how humans and machines interact at the level of the face itself.",
